# Line endings that the INTERPRETER requires, fixed per file type so every
# checkout gets them regardless of each person's core.autocrlf setting.
#
# Windows batch files MUST be CRLF. cmd.exe re-reads a batch file from a
# saved offset after each external command; with LF-only endings that offset
# lands mid-line and cmd executes fragments of later lines -- including REM
# comment text. Measured here: run.bat (then LF) resumed inside its own
# header comment and executed "-> SSRS ...", creating a stray file named SSRS.
# The same comment block carries a pip command, which a mis-resumed launcher
# could RUN -- on machines where the launcher promises it never installs.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts MUST be LF: bash reads a CR as part of each command.
*.sh text eol=lf
